speechtext:
I want to make a last
appeal for the unemployed. Reference has been made to the unemployed in Drumheller. In the last few weeks I have received scores of communications from almost every industrial centre from coast to coast regarding the situation. The Minister of Labour (Mr. Heenan) says to-day with reference to Drumheller, that the men require work and not relief. He should not stop there. If there are in this country large numbers of people who want work and not relief it is the duty of the government to provide that work. It is absurd that we should be spending millions of dollars in an effort to bring men into Canada while there are already in the country thousands who need work. We must recognize that fact. The Minister of Immigration (Mr. Forke) puts it off by suggesting that there may be some little trouble. Let me assure him that it is not a small trouble, nor is it local or temporary in character. It has existed year after year and has brought dire suffering to thousands of families. We may frankly admit that we have a great deal of seasonal labour in Canada, but simply to recognize that fact is not to absolve ourselves from doing anything further. To suggest anything of t'he kind is very wide of the mark. If we have considerable seasonal labour in Canada the Minister of Immigration and the Minister of Labour ought to get together and devise some means of providing employment to those who need it, instead of allowing individuals to suffer. The situation is becoming more and more aggravated by the influx of large'numbers of immigrant's from Europe. I want to read to the Minister of Immigration and the Minister of Labour^ a resolution passed the other day by the city council of Regina, the constituency of the Minister of Railways (Mr. Dunning). It was adopted on April 5, and was forwarded to me by some persons in Regina interested in the situation. It seems to me fairly to state the situation prevailing in a large number of our industrial communities.
Whereas from time to time men seeking employment of a seasonal or casual nature are stranded without visible means of subsistence in our midst, although the labour of these men is of primary importance in the production and harvesting of our field crops and in the development of our country generally:
And whereas the Dominion and provincial authorities by advertisement and the provision of special, low transportation rates to im-
Alberta Coal-Mr. Woodsworth

migrants and men seeking seasonal employment, encourage these men to migrate without seeing that some provision is made for their protection against possible destitution or starvation during unseasonable climatic conditions and periods of unemployment;
And whereas a large number of such men have been arrested in this city and sentenced to varying terms of imprisonment as vagrants, while in some instances such arrests have been made by the police to protect men from the pangs of hunger and the danger of starvation;
And whereas in the opinion of this council the sentencing to jail of persons whose only crime is that of poverty and destitution is inhuman, unjust and anti-social;
Therefore be it resolved that we bring these conditions to the immediate attention of the premiers of the Dominion and of the province, to the federal Ministers of Labour and of Immigration and to the members for Regina in our parliament and legislative assembly, together with the urgent request that steps be taken forthwith to remedy the existing situation and prevent its recurrence.

When we get a
sufficient vote we will consider that matter;
I think we should remember just now that we have to creep before we walk.
